It came from lab-mail's spec and README, which said you keep the
password in pass(1), and from sysutils/password-store in beastie's
ports list. I took those as your setup without asking. Patch 3
corrects both documents.

mbsync has four options:

- Pass <password> in ~/.mbsyncrc, the password in the config itself;
- PassCmd <command>, any command that prints it, such as cat of a
  file or a password manager;
- neither, and mbsync asks for it on each run;
- a SASL or keychain helper, which is overkill for this.

v2 uses PassCmd "cat ~/.config/lab-mail/password". "just install-mail"
makes that file empty and readable only by you, and you write the
password into it once. It is a file, as you suggested, but kept out
of ~/.mbsyncrc so that the config in the repo stays whole and holds
no secret. If you would rather have Pass in ~/.mbsyncrc, it is a
one-line change. msmtp needs no password, since lab-mail takes posts
with no login.

